  "texts": [
    "Why does an espresso machine push water like 90 meters underwater?",
    "Espresso is made at about nine bar of pressure.",
    "That's nine times the air pressure around you.",
    "First, a boiler heats water to about 93 degrees.",
    "Boiling water would pull out too many bitter compounds.",
    "Then a small pump squeezes the hot water.",
    "The coffee is ground to fine powder and packed tight.",
    "That layer resists the water almost like a wall.",
    "Pressure still forces it through in about 25 seconds.",
    "Tiny grains have a huge surface, so flavor dissolves fast.",
    "Pressure also carries out tiny oil droplets, making espresso thick.",
    "Under pressure, more carbon dioxide dissolves into the water.",
    "In the cup, pressure drops and bubbles form the crema.",
    "Just like when you open a bottle of soda.",
    "Follow for more physics hiding in your mug!"
  ]
